  "judgment": {
    "status": "confirmed",
    "reasoning": "All 16 selected answers agree with their own captured Math Academy worked solutions and are mathematically correct. q-98317: option 3 closes back onto its starting point. q-98336: option 0 has no self-intersections and its arrows traverse the boundary counterclockwise. q-70649: the sphere has center (0,-5,0) and squared radius 10. q-164249: completing the square in z gives (x-2)^2+y^2+(z-2)^2=16, hence radius 4; the source incorrectly says it completes the square in y, but its calculation and answer correctly use z. q-73215: setting x=y=0 gives (z-2)^2=1/4, hence z=3/2,5/2. q-71118: setting x=y=0 gives z^2=121/36, hence z=±11/6. q-164310 and q-164399: requiring the expressions under the square roots to be nonnegative gives the stated closed disk and closed elliptical region, respectively; both z branches have these same domains. q-70926: setting x=2 gives (y+4)^2+z^2=12, whose projection has radius 2√3 and center (-4,0) in yz coordinates. q-164179: setting y=0 gives x^2+(z-1)^2=2. q-128445: f(1,0)=1/2 gives x^2+y^2=1. q-36434: f(1,2)=1 gives y=x^2+1. q-129062: c=0 and c=1 give the coordinate axes with the origin excluded; c=2 gives an empty level set because the function takes values only in [0,1]. Thus all three statements are false. q-71398: for c≥0 the curves are y=(c^2-x^2)/2, so only III is true. q-129116: ln(y-x)=c gives y=x+e^c, selecting slope-one lines with positive y-intercepts. q-129112: y-x=c gives y=x+c for every real c, selecting slope-one lines across the whole plane.",
